# False Sharing

## What is False Sharing?

False sharing occurs when two independent variables used by different threads happen to occupy the same cache line.

Example:

```text
Cache Line

┌──────────────────────────────┐
│ counterA │ counterB          │
└──────────────────────────────┘
```

Thread A modifies:

```text
counterA
```

Thread B modifies:

```text
counterB
```

The variables are independent, but the cache line is shared.

## Why Is It Slow?

When one core modifies its variable, the cache line may need to be invalidated or transferred between cores.

Repeated writes create cache-coherence traffic.

```text
Core 1 → modify A
          ↓
       cache line
          ↓
Core 2 → modify B
          ↓
       cache line
```

The cores repeatedly interfere with each other.

## Fix

Separate frequently modified variables into different cache lines.

In C++:

```cpp
struct alignas(64) Counter {
    std::atomic<int> value;
};
```

Another option is explicit padding.

## Important

Do not automatically add padding everywhere.

First:

```text
Measure
 ↓
Identify bottleneck
 ↓
Confirm false sharing
 ↓
Fix
 ↓
Benchmark again
```

## Practice

Create:

```text
Version 1:
counterA and counterB adjacent

Version 2:
counterA and counterB separated
```

Have two threads repeatedly increment them.

Benchmark both versions.

